WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.990
Module 3: the Culture Mosaic.

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:04.665
It's the flagship of the Mosaic family,

00:00:04.665 --> 00:00:09.948
an organization-wide survey that turns
the framework into a designed report a

00:00:09.948 --> 00:00:11.183
client can act on.

00:00:11.183 --> 00:00:16.604
In this module you'll take a Culture
Mosaic from first conversation to finished

00:00:16.604 --> 00:00:21.063
report, and you'll set one up for
Northwind Health along the way.

00:00:21.063 --> 00:00:23.327
It takes about a hundred minutes.

00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:29.468
The Culture Mosaic asks every employee how
they experience the organization across

00:00:29.468 --> 00:00:30.636
the twelve tiles.

00:00:30.636 --> 00:00:32.835
There are fifty-four statements,

00:00:32.835 --> 00:00:37.920
each rated from Strongly Disagree to
Strongly Agree on a five-point scale,

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.050
and it takes about ten minutes.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:45.204
There are two standard open questions:
what will continue to serve us well,

00:00:45.204 --> 00:00:48.708
and where are our biggest
opportunities to improve.

00:00:48.708 --> 00:00:54.205
It also produces a Psychological Safety
Index from four of the fifty-four items,

00:00:54.205 --> 00:00:55.855
with no extra questions.

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.672
It's anonymous by default, with no names,

00:00:58.672 --> 00:01:02.657
email addresses or device
details stored with the answers.

00:01:02.657 --> 00:01:05.887
And the items are written
about "we" and "our",

00:01:05.887 --> 00:01:10.697
so people rate the organization as
they experience it, not themselves.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.085
Each tile is measured
by four or five items.

00:01:14.085 --> 00:01:16.511
Two kinds of item behave differently,

00:01:16.511 --> 00:01:19.854
and you need to know both
before you read a report.

00:01:19.854 --> 00:01:21.756
Two items are reverse-scored.

00:01:21.756 --> 00:01:25.624
They're worded so that agreeing
is the less healthy answer,

00:01:25.624 --> 00:01:28.771
like "Disagreements are
rarely openly explored".

00:01:28.771 --> 00:01:34.278
The engine flips them before scoring, so in
the report a higher bar is still better.

00:01:34.278 --> 00:01:36.376
Don't un-flip them in your head.

00:01:36.376 --> 00:01:38.212
Three items are leader-only.

00:01:38.212 --> 00:01:43.326
Everyone sees them, but only respondents
tagged as leaders are scored on them.

00:01:43.326 --> 00:01:46.211
They sit in Change Ready
and Future Focused,

00:01:46.211 --> 00:01:49.817
and their count is the number
of leaders, not everyone.

00:01:49.817 --> 00:01:54.407
They depend on a working leader
question, which you'll set up shortly.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:55.924
Here's a rule.

00:01:55.924 --> 00:01:58.921
The fifty-four standard items are locked.

00:01:58.921 --> 00:02:01.918
You can't reword, reorder or delete them,

00:02:01.918 --> 00:02:07.986
because the benchmark only works if every
organization answers exactly those words.

00:02:07.986 --> 00:02:11.349
If a client wants to ask
about something else,

00:02:11.349 --> 00:02:16.686
there are two routes: add custom
open-ended questions, or add a subscale.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.841
The Psychological Safety
Index is a readiness signal.

00:02:20.841 --> 00:02:24.414
It asks whether people feel
safe enough to speak up,

00:02:24.414 --> 00:02:26.887
take smart risks and admit mistakes.

00:02:26.887 --> 00:02:31.353
It reuses four items: about
exploring disagreements, speaking up,

00:02:31.353 --> 00:02:35.750
treating mistakes as learning,
and seeking diverse perspectives.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.941
Each respondent's index is the
average of those four answers,

00:02:39.941 --> 00:02:43.033
and it only counts if
they answered all four.

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:46.881
The organization's index is
the average of those scores,

00:02:46.881 --> 00:02:49.835
rounded to a whole
number from one to five,

00:02:49.835 --> 00:02:53.751
and the report shows one of
five index pages based on it.

00:02:53.751 --> 00:02:59.179
Because it's built from items that already
exist, every Culture Mosaic has one.

00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:03.959
Good scoping prevents most of
the problems you'll ever have.

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:06.677
Settle four things before anyone buys.

00:03:06.677 --> 00:03:07.321
Who's in?

00:03:07.321 --> 00:03:12.900
The Culture Mosaic is designed as a
census, so everyone in scope gets invited.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:13.974
How big a tier?

00:03:13.974 --> 00:03:17.479
Tiers count completed
responses, not invitations,

00:03:17.479 --> 00:03:21.342
and the survey stops taking
answers at the tier's cap.

00:03:21.342 --> 00:03:26.063
Estimate responses as invitations
times a realistic response rate,

00:03:26.063 --> 00:03:27.493
then leave headroom.

00:03:27.493 --> 00:03:33.359
Online checkout covers the smaller tiers,
and the Mosaic team sets up larger ones.

00:03:33.359 --> 00:03:34.361
Which add-ons?

00:03:34.361 --> 00:03:35.863
You'll see them next.

00:03:35.863 --> 00:03:36.507
And when?

00:03:36.507 --> 00:03:40.799
The survey must launch within
about two months of the order,

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:45.019
and most organizations run
fieldwork for two to four weeks.

00:03:45.019 --> 00:03:49.669
Ask the sponsor what decision this
will help them make, and when.

00:03:49.669 --> 00:03:52.173
Then work backwards from that date.

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:54.655
Let's size Northwind's order.

00:03:54.655 --> 00:03:58.563
Alex plans to invite all six
hundred and forty employees,

00:03:58.563 --> 00:04:02.470
and their last survey got
about two-thirds participation.

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.527
Which tier should they choose?

00:04:04.527 --> 00:04:09.463
Do the arithmetic, remember what the
tier counts, then pick your answer.

00:04:09.967 --> 00:04:12.223
There are four kinds of add-on.

00:04:12.223 --> 00:04:17.027
First, subscales: Employee Engagement,
DEI Climate, Accessibility,

00:04:17.027 --> 00:04:18.846
and Workplace Resilience.

00:04:18.846 --> 00:04:22.340
Each has its own benchmark,
and each adds items,

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.033
so it adds time for every respondent.

00:04:25.033 --> 00:04:29.691
Choose them because the client
has a real question in that area.

00:04:29.691 --> 00:04:35.150
Second, custom open-ended questions:
up to five, in the client's own words.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.843
The text stays editable until launch.

00:04:37.843 --> 00:04:41.773
Third, demographic breakdowns,
also called subreports.

00:04:41.773 --> 00:04:46.577
A breakdown compares results across
the groups of one demographic,

00:04:46.577 --> 00:04:51.162
like department or tenure, and
it can combine two into one cut,

00:04:51.162 --> 00:04:53.055
like Department by Tenure.

00:04:53.055 --> 00:04:58.222
Each breakdown is bought, and the
report compares only the ones set up.

00:04:58.222 --> 00:05:02.517
With none, the only comparison
is Leader versus Non-Leader.

00:05:02.517 --> 00:05:05.064
A Mosaic team account has no limit,

00:05:05.064 --> 00:05:08.630
and a partner's own code
applies to the purchase.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:14.599
Fourth, an executive readout: a ninety-minute
session in which a Mosaic consultant

00:05:14.599 --> 00:05:17.728
walks the client's team
through the report.

00:05:18.233 --> 00:05:22.623
Set expectations to match the report
the client will actually open.

00:05:22.623 --> 00:05:24.851
Three add-ons can surprise people.

00:05:24.851 --> 00:05:30.224
The client's own questions appear in a
"Your Questions" section at the back of the

00:05:30.224 --> 00:05:30.551
deck.

00:05:30.551 --> 00:05:34.941
Each open-ended question gets its
own slide with summarised themes.

00:05:34.941 --> 00:05:40.248
Choice and rating questions are drawn as
charts, for the whole organization only.

00:05:40.248 --> 00:05:45.228
And a question fewer than five people
answered shows only how many answered,

00:05:45.228 --> 00:05:47.325
so no one's words can be traced.

00:05:47.325 --> 00:05:51.321
Comparison slides cover only
the breakdowns that were set up.

00:05:51.321 --> 00:05:54.335
With none, it's Leader
versus Non-Leader only.

00:05:54.335 --> 00:05:57.153
Combined cuts are drawn
as combined groups,

00:05:57.153 --> 00:06:01.018
and combinations too small to
show are pooled into "Other".

00:06:01.018 --> 00:06:06.064
And subscale sections appear only when
the subscale was bought and turned on.

00:06:06.567 --> 00:06:10.725
Here's what the buyer sees between
paying and having a survey.

00:06:10.725 --> 00:06:13.743
At checkout they choose
the tier and add-ons,

00:06:13.743 --> 00:06:17.097
and answer the research-database
consent question,

00:06:17.097 --> 00:06:21.657
which decides whether the organization
may join the benchmark later.

00:06:21.657 --> 00:06:26.956
After payment, they get a claim link
that's single-use and lasts fourteen days.

00:06:26.956 --> 00:06:31.919
The setup wizard asks them to name the
engagement and press Create survey.

00:06:31.919 --> 00:06:37.419
That copies the Culture Mosaic master into
their organization with its protections

00:06:37.419 --> 00:06:38.156
already on.

00:06:38.156 --> 00:06:41.845
Then a confirmation screen
says the first step is done,

00:06:41.845 --> 00:06:46.741
and lists what happens next: open the
survey, work through the checklist,

00:06:46.741 --> 00:06:48.351
then publish and invite.

00:06:48.351 --> 00:06:51.637
One tip: agree in advance
who buys and claims it,

00:06:51.637 --> 00:06:56.869
because the person who claims it becomes
the owner of the client's SurveyCraft

00:06:56.869 --> 00:06:57.741
organization.

00:06:58.233 --> 00:07:03.183
The pre-launch checklist on the survey's
Settings page is your backbone,

00:07:03.183 --> 00:07:04.695
and the order matters.

00:07:04.695 --> 00:07:06.964
Review the demographic questions,

00:07:06.964 --> 00:07:11.363
so the answer choices match the
organization's real departments,

00:07:11.363 --> 00:07:13.288
locations and tenure ranges.

00:07:13.288 --> 00:07:16.107
Add text for custom questions, if bought.

00:07:16.107 --> 00:07:18.306
Configure the result breakdowns.

00:07:18.306 --> 00:07:19.819
Turn on any subscales.

00:07:19.819 --> 00:07:22.912
Customise the welcome
and thank-you messages.

00:07:22.912 --> 00:07:25.731
And take the survey yourself, end to end.

00:07:25.731 --> 00:07:30.268
Then publish, which turns the survey
on without contacting anyone.

00:07:30.268 --> 00:07:31.780
Then invite your team.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:35.630
Everything up to the test run
shapes what people answer,

00:07:35.630 --> 00:07:37.829
so finish it before you publish.

00:07:37.829 --> 00:07:43.398
Taking the survey yourself catches typos
in department names, a missing location,

00:07:43.398 --> 00:07:46.697
and a welcome message that
still says "Company".

00:07:46.697 --> 00:07:49.172
Five minutes here saves a re-launch.

00:07:49.667 --> 00:07:55.052
Every comparison page in the report comes
from the demographic questions and the

00:07:55.052 --> 00:07:56.803
breakdowns set up on them.

00:07:56.803 --> 00:08:01.246
Get them right and the report
answers the client's real questions.

00:08:01.246 --> 00:08:04.343
Get them wrong and no
analysis will rescue it.

00:08:04.343 --> 00:08:07.372
Design the demographic
block with the client.

00:08:07.372 --> 00:08:09.122
Use their real categories.

00:08:09.122 --> 00:08:11.209
Keep groups big enough to show,

00:08:11.209 --> 00:08:16.460
by combining small units up front rather
than leaving them to land in "Other".

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:21.913
Ask only what you'll use, because every
demographic question is one more reason a

00:08:21.913 --> 00:08:25.751
cautious respondent might
think they could be identified.

00:08:25.751 --> 00:08:31.271
And if you use a roster, keep its columns
consistent with the survey's categories.

00:08:31.271 --> 00:08:34.974
Ask the sponsor which groups
they most need to compare,

00:08:34.974 --> 00:08:38.542
and which comparisons would
be too sensitive to show.

00:08:39.033 --> 00:08:43.063
The Culture Mosaic needs to
know who counts as a leader,

00:08:43.063 --> 00:08:46.300
for two reasons: the
three leader-only items,

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.466
and the Leader versus
Non-Leader comparison.

00:08:49.466 --> 00:08:54.215
In Settings, pick one demographic
question, usually level or role,

00:08:54.215 --> 00:08:57.237
and tick the answers that count as leader.

00:08:57.237 --> 00:08:59.971
Agree the definition with the sponsor.

00:08:59.971 --> 00:09:04.432
In one organization, "leader"
means anyone who manages people.

00:09:04.432 --> 00:09:07.022
In another, it's director and above.

00:09:07.022 --> 00:09:12.778
And watch out: if no leader question is
set, or the chosen answers match nobody,

00:09:12.778 --> 00:09:16.232
the leader-only items have
no one to score them.

00:09:16.733 --> 00:09:21.999
People tell the truth when they trust
that nobody can pick out their answers.

00:09:21.999 --> 00:09:26.444
Know every protection, because
clients will ask you to bend them.

00:09:26.444 --> 00:09:29.726
Bought Culture Mosaics
are anonymous by default.

00:09:29.726 --> 00:09:35.060
A roster tracks who has completed, but
answers can't be joined back to people.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:37.864
The minimum group size defaults to three.

00:09:37.864 --> 00:09:40.257
A smaller group rolls into "Other",

00:09:40.257 --> 00:09:43.608
and you can raise the
minimum for sensitive cuts.

00:09:43.608 --> 00:09:47.096
Any report needs at least
five completed responses.

00:09:47.096 --> 00:09:50.788
A subteam report needs at
least ten, with no override.

00:09:50.788 --> 00:09:52.293
Why ten for a subteam?

00:09:52.293 --> 00:09:56.601
A subteam report puts a small
group's results on its own pages,

00:09:56.601 --> 00:10:00.294
with no wider organization
around them, and below ten,

00:10:00.294 --> 00:10:02.824
individuals become too easy to infer.

00:10:02.824 --> 00:10:06.038
When a leader asks for
"just my team of seven",

00:10:06.038 --> 00:10:10.414
the honest answer is that seven
isn't enough for its own report,

00:10:10.414 --> 00:10:14.107
though their results still
count in the whole picture.

00:10:14.107 --> 00:10:17.595
And never lower the minimum
group size below three.

00:10:17.595 --> 00:10:19.715
Leave it at three, or raise it.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.041
Now drag the launch steps
into the right order.

00:10:23.041 --> 00:10:26.546
Everything that shapes the
questions comes before Publish,

00:10:26.546 --> 00:10:29.447
and the report waits until
the survey is closed.

00:10:29.933 --> 00:10:35.229
Distribution happens in two moves:
publish, then choose how people get it.

00:10:35.229 --> 00:10:39.451
The recommended way is to upload
a contact list, or roster,

00:10:39.451 --> 00:10:42.099
of up to twenty-five thousand people.

00:10:42.099 --> 00:10:46.966
Each person gets a personal link,
demographics attach automatically,

00:10:46.966 --> 00:10:52.190
you can track each person's status,
and you can send automatic reminders.

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:54.838
You can also share a link or QR code,

00:10:54.838 --> 00:10:58.416
which suits frontline staff
without company email.

00:10:58.416 --> 00:11:03.211
Or send email invitations, for
smaller groups without a clean list.

00:11:03.211 --> 00:11:08.149
Many organizations combine methods:
a roster for everyone with email,

00:11:08.149 --> 00:11:11.655
and a QR code in break
rooms for frontline teams.

00:11:11.655 --> 00:11:13.588
Northwind did exactly that.

00:11:13.588 --> 00:11:18.168
And rosters protect anonymity:
they show that someone completed,

00:11:18.168 --> 00:11:19.670
never what they said.

00:11:20.167 --> 00:11:21.817
Here's the roster flow.

00:11:21.817 --> 00:11:26.122
On the Distribute tab, download
the template, fill in names,

00:11:26.122 --> 00:11:29.710
emails and any demographic
columns, and upload it.

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:31.934
Fix any rows the preview flags.

00:11:31.934 --> 00:11:37.460
Send invitations, and each person's
status moves from Not invited to Invited.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:39.827
Then turn on automatic reminders.

00:11:39.827 --> 00:11:45.496
Choose an interval of every three, five
or seven days, and one to three rounds.

00:11:45.496 --> 00:11:50.304
Reminders go only to people who
were invited and haven't completed,

00:11:50.304 --> 00:11:54.178
and none starts within twelve
hours of the close date.

00:11:54.178 --> 00:11:58.771
A reminder about a week in typically
lifts responses noticeably.

00:11:58.771 --> 00:12:04.726
Pair it with a note from a senior leader
explaining why the survey matters and that

00:12:04.726 --> 00:12:05.803
it's anonymous.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:09.280
While the survey is
live, watch three things.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:10.936
First, the response rate.

00:12:10.936 --> 00:12:15.506
Track completions against invitations,
and if you distribute by link,

00:12:15.506 --> 00:12:17.758
set the invited count in Settings.

00:12:17.758 --> 00:12:20.672
Otherwise the report
will say "Not tracked".

00:12:20.672 --> 00:12:22.461
Second, the respondent cap.

00:12:22.461 --> 00:12:27.031
The owner gets an email at eighty
percent and at one hundred percent,

00:12:27.031 --> 00:12:30.408
and at one hundred the
survey stops taking answers.

00:12:30.408 --> 00:12:31.998
Third, the upgrade path.

00:12:31.998 --> 00:12:37.495
If a survey is filling up, the owner can
buy the next tier from the Responses page.

00:12:37.495 --> 00:12:41.734
At Northwind, the eighty percent
email arrived on day seventeen.

00:12:41.734 --> 00:12:45.046
The team checked the pace,
didn't need to upgrade,

00:12:45.046 --> 00:12:48.357
and finished on four hundred
and twelve responses.

00:12:48.867 --> 00:12:50.787
Remember the launch deadline.

00:12:50.787 --> 00:12:55.950
A bought Culture Mosaic must leave Draft
within about two months of the order.

00:12:55.950 --> 00:13:00.518
SurveyCraft sends warning emails
fourteen days and three days before.

00:13:00.518 --> 00:13:04.093
An unlaunched survey that
misses the deadline expires,

00:13:04.093 --> 00:13:07.801
and staff then decide with
the client whether to extend.

00:13:07.801 --> 00:13:11.045
Plan the launch date before
the order, not after.

00:13:11.533 --> 00:13:16.253
You end data collection in one of
two ways: close the survey by hand,

00:13:16.253 --> 00:13:18.373
or set an automatic close date.

00:13:18.373 --> 00:13:21.315
You can reopen if you
need a few more days.

00:13:21.315 --> 00:13:23.846
Before you close, check three things.

00:13:23.846 --> 00:13:26.240
Has the response rate levelled off?

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:30.138
A last reminder two or three
days before close is common.

00:13:30.138 --> 00:13:33.490
Are the groups you care
about big enough to show?

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:36.226
And have you deleted any test responses?

00:13:36.226 --> 00:13:40.399
They're excluded anyway, but
clean data is easier to explain.

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:44.973
On the Responses tab, choose
Generate Culture Mosaic Report.

00:13:44.973 --> 00:13:47.891
You need at least five
completed responses.

00:13:47.891 --> 00:13:53.593
It runs in the background for a minute or
three, and you're emailed when it's ready.

00:13:53.593 --> 00:13:54.814
Download the deck.

00:13:54.814 --> 00:13:58.751
It's an editable PowerPoint,
and it's kept for seven days,

00:13:58.751 --> 00:14:01.195
so store it with the client's files.

00:14:01.195 --> 00:14:06.150
The Responses tab also offers a subteam
report for groups of ten or more,

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:09.951
a comparison with an earlier
survey, and an action plan.

00:14:09.951 --> 00:14:14.023
The deck includes the framework
pages, the headline diamond,

00:14:14.023 --> 00:14:16.670
item detail with percentiles, heatmaps,

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:20.743
comparisons for each breakdown
and Leader versus Non-Leader,

00:14:20.743 --> 00:14:25.630
themes from the open questions, the
safety index, any subscale sections,

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:27.870
and "Your Questions" at the back.

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:30.585
You'll learn to read it all in Module 8.

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:33.347
Here's a rule worth remembering.

00:14:33.347 --> 00:14:38.612
A report generated before the survey
is closed does not join the benchmark.

00:14:38.612 --> 00:14:42.613
Joining happens when the report
is generated after close.

00:14:42.613 --> 00:14:46.756
Even then, a survey stays out
if the owner switched it off,

00:14:46.756 --> 00:14:52.512
the buyer declined consent, it's an internal
account, a subteam report, test data,

00:14:52.512 --> 00:14:54.267
or under ten respondents.

00:14:54.267 --> 00:14:57.918
If a client needs an early
look, generate a preview,

00:14:57.918 --> 00:15:01.428
then close and generate
again when fieldwork ends.

00:15:01.933 --> 00:15:07.005
The executive readout is a ninety-minute
session with a Mosaic consultant,

00:15:07.005 --> 00:15:12.008
who walks the client's team through
the report and helps plan next steps.

00:15:12.008 --> 00:15:16.531
If you're delivering the debrief
yourself, Module 9 shows you how.

00:15:16.531 --> 00:15:20.849
Either way, never send a sixty-slide
deck to an executive cold.

00:15:20.849 --> 00:15:24.550
A report without a conversation
gets skimmed, misread,

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:26.880
or used to hunt for who said what.

00:15:27.367 --> 00:15:28.767
Now it's your turn.

00:15:28.767 --> 00:15:31.126
You're Northwind's practitioner,

00:15:31.126 --> 00:15:36.950
and Alex has claimed the order: a census
of six hundred and forty people with a

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:38.867
five-hundred-response cap.

00:15:38.867 --> 00:15:44.543
Work through eight decisions, from the
first move after setup to wrapping up.

00:15:44.543 --> 00:15:48.524
Some choices are critical
errors that end the attempt,

00:15:48.524 --> 00:15:51.399
and you can retry as often as you like.

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:57.114
Time for the module check: twelve questions,
and you need ten right to move on.

00:15:57.114 --> 00:16:01.800
You can retake it as often as you
like, with fresh questions each time.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:07.475
Here's your Culture Mosaic Setup Checklist,
from scoping through close and report.

00:16:07.475 --> 00:16:10.189
Download it and use it
on every engagement.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:11.347
To recap.

00:16:11.347 --> 00:16:15.086
Scope first: tiers count
responses, not invitations,

00:16:15.086 --> 00:16:18.752
and the survey must launch
within about two months.

00:16:18.752 --> 00:16:22.994
Do everything that shapes the
questions before you publish.

00:16:22.994 --> 00:16:28.817
Protect anonymity: a minimum group of
three, which you can raise but never lower,

00:16:28.817 --> 00:16:33.131
five responses for any report,
and ten for a subteam report.

00:16:33.131 --> 00:16:38.092
Roster where you can, add a link or
QR code for people without email,

00:16:38.092 --> 00:16:39.673
and turn on reminders.

00:16:39.673 --> 00:16:43.699
Close, then generate, so the
client joins the benchmark.

00:16:43.699 --> 00:16:48.660
And set expectations to match the
deck the client will actually open.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:51.895
Next: Module 4, the
Culture Progress Monitor.

